1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:15,300
Episode 7 used jal and jr ra. In fact, RISC-V has only two real unconditional jump instructions.

2
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:25,000
jal rd, label stands for "jump and link," but it really links first: it writes PC + 4 into rd, then jumps to label.

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:34,700
jalr rd, rs1, imm also links first, but jumps to rs1 + imm: the address comes from a register.

4
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:46,266
Every other jump is a pseudoinstruction: j, jr and ret write the link to x0, discarding it; jal without rd links to ra.

5
00:00:46,266 --> 00:00:52,900
Why two? jal's target is fixed at assembly time, which is just right for calling a function by name.

6
00:00:52,900 --> 00:01:05,866
jalr's target comes from a register at run time. That covers returning to the address in ra, calling through a function pointer, and, with auipc, jumping far.

7
00:01:05,866 --> 00:01:16,566
This episode's star is factorial, defined by factorial(1) = 1 and factorial(n) = n × factorial(n − 1).

8
00:01:16,566 --> 00:01:23,833
What if we save nothing? Say main calls factorial(2): a0 = 2, and ra holds the way back to main.

9
00:01:23,833 --> 00:01:32,366
factorial(2) puts the argument 1 in a0 and does a jal: now ra points back into factorial(2).

10
00:01:32,366 --> 00:01:44,900
factorial(1) returns 1, but factorial(2)'s 2 was overwritten by the argument 1; and ra still points into factorial(2), so it can never get back to main.

11
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:52,233
The lazy fix: on every call, push all of x1 through x31, then load them all back afterward.

12
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:58,233
But memory is slow, and a call usually needs to protect only a few registers.

13
00:01:58,233 --> 00:02:09,966
The fix is Episode 7's calling convention: callers can rely only on s registers and sp; a callee that uses an s register saves it first and restores it before returning.

14
00:02:09,966 --> 00:02:16,800
A recursive function plays both roles: callee to the level above, caller to the level below.

15
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:26,666
Here is the notes' factorial, which follows the calling convention, starting at address 0x2000. First, spot the prologue and epilogue.

16
00:02:26,666 --> 00:02:38,066
The prologue carves out 8 bytes on the stack: this call's stack frame. The jal further down will overwrite ra, and s0 is borrowed, so its old value is saved too.

17
00:02:38,066 --> 00:02:47,333
mv s0 a0 copies n into s0. s registers survive calls, so n is still there when the recursive call returns.

18
00:02:47,333 --> 00:02:56,266
bne compares two registers, so li first puts 1 in t0. If n ≠ 1, branch to recurse.

19
00:02:56,266 --> 00:03:07,300
Otherwise it's the base case: a0 = 1. It can't just jr ra: s0 isn't restored and the frame isn't popped yet, so it jumps to the epilogue.

20
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:19,600
recurse: a0 = n − 1, and it calls itself. On return a0 = (n − 1)!, which is multiplied by n from s0.

21
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:30,900
The epilogue runs in the opposite order: load ra and s0 back from the stack, then add 8 to sp to pop the frame, and finally jr ra.

22
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:41,766
Let's trace factorial(3) all the way through. main has already allocated a 12-byte frame, so sp = 0xFFFFFFD4.

23
00:03:41,766 --> 00:03:52,366
main puts 3 in a0 and executes the jal at 0x1004: ra = 0x1008, and control enters factorial.

24
00:03:52,366 --> 00:04:05,566
factorial(3)'s prologue: sp drops by 8 to 0xFFFFFFCC, then it saves ra = 0x1008 and main's s0 = 42.

25
00:04:05,566 --> 00:04:19,900
mv sets s0 = 3. Since 3 ≠ 1, it branches to recurse: a0 = 2, and the jal at 0x2024 sets ra = 0x2028.

26
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:35,300
factorial(2) pushes a frame the same way: sp = 0xFFFFFFC4, saving ra = 0x2028 and s0 = 3, then calls itself with a0 = 1.

27
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:47,666
factorial(1) pushes one too: sp = 0xFFFFFFBC, saving ra = 0x2028 and s0 = 2.

28
00:04:47,666 --> 00:04:59,700
Each of the three frames holds its own ra and s0. factorial(3)'s ra leads back to main; the other two lead to the mul at 0x2028.

29
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:06,166
factorial(1) is the base case: a0 = 1, then j to the epilogue.

30
00:05:06,166 --> 00:05:18,966
The epilogue restores ra = 0x2028 and s0 = 2, sp goes back to 0xFFFFFFC4, and jr ra returns to the mul.

31
00:05:18,966 --> 00:05:34,100
a0 = s0 × a0 = 2 × 1 = 2. The 2 in s0 is factorial(2)'s own n: factorial(1) used s0, but restored it before returning.

32
00:05:34,100 --> 00:05:50,533
factorial(2) runs its epilogue: ra = 0x2028, s0 = 3, sp = 0xFFFFFFCC. Back at the mul: a0 = 3 × 2 = 6.

33
00:05:50,533 --> 00:06:03,766
factorial(3) restores ra = 0x1008 and main's s0 = 42, sp goes back to 0xFFFFFFD4, and it returns to main.

34
00:06:03,766 --> 00:06:15,000
Back in main: a0 = 6, and sp and s0 are just as they were before the call. The popped frames are still in memory; nobody uses them anymore.

35
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:25,300
The notes' foo is recursive too: foo(i) = i + foo(i − 1). Put it next to factorial and the skeleton is identical.

36
00:06:25,300 --> 00:06:34,866
Only three things differ: it compares against x0 directly, saving an li; the base case returns 0; and multiply becomes add.

37
00:06:34,866 --> 00:06:49,200
The notes' main keeps foo(3) in s0, where it survives the call to foo(100). At its deepest, foo(100) has 101 frames on the stack: 808 bytes.

38
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:56,633
Not every function needs the stack. In the notes, sum_square calls mult twice, and mult calls nothing at all.

39
00:06:56,633 --> 00:07:09,800
A function like mult is a leaf function: with no jal, ra is never overwritten, and if it uses only t and a registers, it needs no sw or lw at all.

40
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:17,733
Here's the full register convention table. gp and tp have special jobs outside the calling convention, so leave them alone.

41
00:07:17,733 --> 00:07:27,566
s0 is also called fp, the frame pointer: sp may move partway through a function, but fp keeps pointing at the current frame.

42
00:07:27,566 --> 00:07:34,666
The notes' func_a moves sp partway through: it calls func_b and needs t1 both before and after the call.

43
00:07:34,666 --> 00:07:43,900
The prologue pushes 8 bytes and saves ra and s0. ra is caller-saved, but by convention it is saved in the prologue anyway.

44
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:55,100
t1 = 10, s0 = 20. t1 is caller-saved and func_b may change it, so before the call, func_a pushes 4 more bytes to save t1.

45
00:07:55,100 --> 00:08:05,033
After func_b returns, t1 could hold anything. Luckily the stack has a copy: t1 = 10 comes back, and those 4 bytes are popped right away.

46
00:08:05,033 --> 00:08:13,966
When sp moves, the offsets move with it: a moment ago 0(sp) was t1; only after the pop is it ra again.

47
00:08:13,966 --> 00:08:27,200
Finally t1 = 15 and s0 = 25. The epilogue restores ra and the caller's s0 = 42, puts sp back, and ret returns.

48
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:33,466
Three true-or-false questions from the notes' exercises. Think them over first.

49
00:08:33,466 --> 00:08:41,633
1 is false: a0 and a1 carry return values back. Like the t registers, the a registers are caller-saved.

50
00:08:41,633 --> 00:08:48,666
2 is true: s registers are callee-saved, usually saved in the prologue and restored in the epilogue.

51
00:08:48,666 --> 00:08:57,266
3 is false: func_a pushed t1 partway through. As long as every push has a matching pop, the stack can be used anywhere.

52
00:08:57,266 --> 00:09:09,266
Finally, Episode 7's six steps once more. Steps 1 and 2 belong to the caller: save any t and a registers it still needs, set up the arguments, then jal.

53
00:09:09,266 --> 00:09:19,100
Step 3, the prologue: lower sp once, making room for local arrays too; save the s registers it uses, and ra if it calls others.

54
00:09:19,100 --> 00:09:29,933
Step 4 is the body. Steps 5 and 6 are the epilogue: return value in a0, restore registers, pop the frame, jr ra.

55
00:09:29,933 --> 00:09:39,333
The notes' analogy: a call is like house-sitting for your parents. They're the caller, you're the callee; the table is the registers, the closet is memory.

56
00:09:39,333 --> 00:09:49,733
The arguments are the essentials on the table. Stash the rest in the closet (prologue); before leaving, put it back and leave a gift: the return value (epilogue).

